CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 10
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ address of pack static member has non-varargs type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ the pack-parameter function is callable: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*invariant violation.*$
^.*symbol type must match.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
[temp.variadic]/3 + [dcl.fct]: a `...` immediately following a parameter
declaration with no separating comma (`f(Args...)`) introduces a function
parameter pack, not a C-style variadic ellipsis.  CBMC parsed an end-of-list
`Args...` as the parameter `Args` plus a separate C-style ellipsis, giving the
instantiated function type a spurious variadic `...`; taking the address of
such a member produced a type that mismatched a concrete function pointer and
triggered an invariant violation.  This is the shape of libstdc++'s
_Function_handler<R(Args...), F>::_M_invoke used by std::function.
